{"qid":"Q762943","names":["County of Holland"],"title":"County of Holland","endonym":null,"capsule":"The County of Holland grew from a Carolingian frontier district in the Rhine-Maas delta into the leading power of the northern Low Countries under the Gerulfing and Dirk (Theodoric) comital line, its wealth built on the herring trade, reclaimed polders, and rising towns such as Haarlem, Leiden, and Dordrecht. Count Floris V (r. 1256–1296) extended control over West Frisia and cultivated the towns and lesser knights before his murder by disaffected nobles in 1296, after which the county passed to the House of Avesnes, counts also of Hainaut. By the mid-fourteenth century the union with Hainaut and the contested succession following William IV's death in 1345 ushered in the factional struggle between the Hook and Cod parties, setting nobility against the increasingly assertive towns.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The county's core territory is well documented through charters and chronicles; outer boundaries, especially in Frisian areas, expanded and contracted and are mapped with moderate precision.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"County of Holland","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Gerolf","nativeName":"Gerulf","roman":"Gerolf","start":885,"end":896,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Dirk I","nativeName":"Dirk I","roman":"Dirk I","start":896,"end":931,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Dirk II","nativeName":"Dirk II","roman":"Dirk II","start":931,"end":988,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLice